{
  "version": 270,
  "released": "2026-08-30",
  "changelog": "v270: EVENT IDENTITY, PHASE I3 — THE QUESTIONS. I0 settled what a record means, I1 taught the fold to apply one, I2 taught the substrate to DECIDE one as data. This is the first phase a PERSON'S ANSWER reaches. The controlling design is lifehug-platform `docs/design/event-identity.md` v4 (§6.1 same_event's five answers, §6.3 possible_overmerge's four, §5.5 the split rules table, §6.4 the listener leaf, the §13.3/§13.4 promises); this repo's summary is ADR 0031, updated to say I3 shipped.\n\nSAME_EVENT AND POSSIBLE_OVERMERGE ARE NOW REGISTERED IN WORK_ITEM_KINDS. I2 deliberately left them unregistered and tested the absence by name; `tests/test_event_identity_i2_binder.py`'s guard is now a positive assertion, because something can finally file an answer. Both kinds get value-scoring defaults and surfaces beside the five existing kinds (`temporal_timeline.WORK_ITEM_VALUE_DEFAULTS`/`SURFACES_BY_KIND`), sit in `WORK_ITEM_PRECEDENCE` just below `contradiction`, and join Mirror's allowlist (`mirror_work.MIRROR_WORK_ITEM_KINDS`) so every actionable Mirror row keeps having Play. `episode_binder.same_event_work_items` and `possible_overmerge_work_items` are the pure functions that turn I2's pairwise outputs (question rows, the overmerge audit, a re-audit's mint) into ordinary `TemporalWorkItem`s through the SAME scoring formula every other kind uses (`temporal_timeline.work_item_score`, a new public door onto the private one) — never a priority of their own, exactly as §4.1 requires. Wiring that generation into the published `work-items.json` loop is a platform (I-P) concern; this phase proves the conversion is correct and pure.\n\nTHE PROBE NAMES BOTH SIDES. `timeline_interaction.WORK_ITEM_PROBES` gains `same_event` (\"Is “{telling_quote}” the same thing as “{episode_quote}”{cast}?\") and `possible_overmerge`, both threaded through two new optional keys on `work_item_target`'s normalization (`telling_quote`/`episode_quote`, plus `cast`) so every other kind's `.format()` call is untouched. The FIVE ANSWERS are a CLOSED CHOICE, not free text this module parses — the same shape `timeline-move --relation` already uses, never a second `same_event` content parser.\n\nA NEW MODULE, `identity_questions.py`, IS I3'S HOME. `resolve_same_event_answer` files Same (a confirmed `same` binding, superseding a state-side proposal via the origin-transition rule — but ONLY when the existing record is machine-origin, or a repeated human answer would chain a fresh supersession onto itself every time), Part of it (confirmed `part_of` — already admitted by `validate_event_identity` at confirmed/stated origin; no C2 validator amendment was needed, only `deterministic` is pinned to `same`), Related (confirmed `related`), Different (confirmed `not_same`, pair-permanent), and Not sure (NO binding — `unknown` asserts nothing about the world, so it is a durable bookkeeping deferral under `state/`, never a `sources/identity/` record). A SAME_EVENT PAIR'S CANDIDATE MAY NOT EXIST YET: `episode_binder.prospective_episode_id` keys a pair on the id an `authority: deterministic` create WOULD mint, but R1 already declined it — nothing deterministic would ever re-derive it — so a human's Same answer files an `authority: human` create instead, a legitimately DIFFERENT id, and `_episode_exists` asks the vault rather than trusting a caller's flag. `resolve_possible_overmerge_answer` dispatches keep together (writes NOTHING — `FORBIDDEN_REAUDIT_ACTIONS` already refuses a system confirm, and a person confirming an untouched bind is not an identity write), fix the date (writes nothing, names the next step — a date correction, never an identity write), part of (supersedes the same binding with `part_of`), and split (`split_episode`).\n\nTHE SPLIT GESTURE WRITES, FOR THE FIRST TIME. `split_episode` files one `split` envelope — each departing telling's prior `same`/`part_of` binding named in `supersedes_binding_ids` and re-filed alongside (a no-op, create-or-keep), a `SPLIT_DEPARTURE_RELATION` (\"none\") binding per departure, and a fresh `same` binding for every telling routed to a NEW episode created in the same envelope — then calls C4's `split_routing` (already fully specified and tested at I0) over the caller's references. Proven end-to-end on all seven reference kinds: an ordering constraint and a bare \"other_decision\" both become Mirror-judgment residue rows (§5.5's catch-all, never a guess), era membership/display/label/work-item/open-session references each route per their own rule, the survivor keeps its id, and replay is a no-op.\n\n\"NOT SURE\" COOLS DOWN AND REOPENS EARLY. `episode_routing_contract` (C4's own home) gains `defer_pair`/`cooldown_active`/`material_new_evidence` — pure functions, `DEFERRAL_COOLDOWN_DAYS = 90` per §12 ruling 3 — and `identity_questions` persists the deferral under `state/temporal_claims/identity_deferrals.json`, a projection safe to delete: the record of having asked is rebuildable bookkeeping about a QUESTION, never a durable decision about the world. A pair with genuinely new evidence on either side (the caller's own snapshot comparison) reopens before the 90 days elapse; an unchanged snapshot does not.\n\nTHE LISTENER HEARS IDENTITY TOO (§6.4, an ADR 0029 amendment — audit B4: ordinary conversation is a source too). `general_listener.Heard` gains a FOURTH typed list, `identity_assertions`, at the END, exactly the v229 `claims` precedent: `{telling_hint, episode_hint, relation}` — MENTIONS, never refs — resolved by `parse_identity_assertions` against a CANDIDATE-CONTEXT excerpt the HOST supplies, never the model's own guess. Zero or two matches per hint is a TYPED REFUSAL, not a guess, and clears `listener_heard_nothing`'s backstop exactly as `DROPPED_NON_FAMILY` does: retrying would not disambiguate a candidate set that genuinely does not. A resolved assertion IS a thing heard, beside a landmark record or a claim. `bind_identity_assertions` is the filing half — `origin: \"stated\"` (spontaneous conversation, never `confirmed`, which is reserved for a directed `same_event` answer) — and every write still goes through `event_identity`'s own writers. `record_answer`/`listen_to_answer` thread an `identity_candidates` parameter end to end; every pre-I3 caller is byte-identical because the parameter defaults to empty. The leaf (`interactions/landmarks/prompt/listener.md`) gained its own section and two new derived substitutions, `{identity_relations}` and `{identity_candidates}`.\n\nTWO NEW VERBS, CLASSIFIED LIKE THEIR SIBLING. `resolve-work-item --kind {same_event,possible_overmerge} --answer ...` and `split-episode --episode-id ... --destination telling_ref=standalone|episode:<hex>` join `DIRECT_MUTATION_COMMANDS` beside `bind-episodes` — both write through the same envelope/binding writers, so they take the vault mutation lease the same way. Neither runs inside `compile`, swept the same way `episode_binder` itself is (`test_identity_questions_never_runs_inside_compile`). Rollout is unchanged: the weekly maintenance step stays `bind-episodes --dry-run` by construction — I3 built the confirmation, split and possible_overmerge flows §8 required before any live founder bind, but the owner's reviewed dry-run and the all-tenant backfill runbook are still I-P's to build.\n\nPROVEN TO FIRE. Every negative below was run against a build with its guard removed and SEEN failing first: a repeated human answer chaining a fresh supersession onto itself; a split's envelope missing its prior bindings (`identity_envelope_incomplete`); a departing telling's `same` binding surviving a split; a Not-sure cooldown never expiring; an unchanged evidence snapshot reopening a deferral early; an ambiguous listener hint silently resolving to one candidate anyway; a two-match hint resolving instead of refusing; an unknown relation slipping past the leaf's closed vocabulary; a no-action re-audit still minting a work item; an unsurfaced same_event row minting one anyway; the CLI's `--destination` flag accepting zero destinations on `split-episode`.",
